HekateBook1: {
	Page1:
		'''
		Idri's been concerning me as of late. I've had to treat hives
		on his skin day-in and day-out. He hasnt been able to meet with
		the group for weeks, citing a newfound lethargy, and it's been
		detrimental to the planning some of our more ambitious plans,
		since he tended to be our voice of reason.
		'''
	Page2:
		'''
		I write this as I lay beside him in bed. He's fallen asleep, but
		the restless shuddering of his torso tells me he isn't sleeping easy.
		I've gotten him soups from all varieties of market, but nothing cures
		his mysterious aliment, and no text could ever categorize diseases
		affecting demideities.
		'''
	Page3:
		'''
		I may consult mother darkness on the matter, but I'm hesitant. 
		Justifiedly so. She would be furious if she knew it had been me
		that had taken her son from her, and I get the feeling her
		mercy to old friends only extends so far. Goodness, I don't
		know how many other options I have.
		'''
}

HekateBook2: {
	Page1:
		'''
		Idri's aliment seems to have become a particular issue. He's left his
		quarters less frequently, and his hives have spread to his arms.
		He has begun to refuse my help in treating the hives, but has not
		said why. Unfortunately, I must do what I can to help him, and have had
		to restrain him on multiple occasions to apply the ointment.
		'''
	Page2: ""
	Page3: ""
}

HekateBook3: {
	Page1:
		'''
		I wept for the first time in centuries this morning. And not the
		usual sob or choke-up. I had gotten a new understaning of mortality
		as I lay against the wall, wailing a discordant chime and clenching
		my eyes shut. It was as if I didnt want to face the reality that
		already had turned to face me.
		'''
	Page2:
		'''
		As I had predicted, yet so foolishly ignored, Idri's breathing has
		become more strained, and his gangrene has worsened depsite my
		regenerative talismans. Foolish of me. Demigod or not, I should have
		stayed by his side all through that night instead of leaving him
		in this cold, lonely room.
		'''
	Page3:
		'''
		I haven't felt things so rawly in so long a time. It perplexes me
		as much as it moves me. Deities are beyond emotion; that's a commonly
		known fact. So how is it that I grieve? How is it that I weep so loud
		and so long that my chest throbs sorely and my eyes are burned by
		my caustic fluids? Perhaps we need to rethink our defintions of
		Deitism.
		'''
}
